      "name": "pink primrose",
      "hint": "Identity grounding for this class: pink primrose = pink evening primrose, Oenothera speciosa, not a pink-flowered Primula. Typical flowers have FOUR broad overlapping petals, a shallow cup or dish shape, pink to white coloration with pink/red veins and a yellowish throat, EIGHT stamens, and a conspicuous FOUR-branched stigma. Flowers are solitary in leaf axils on slender stems; leaves are alternate. Pink/red venation may be visible, not necessarily absent. These are typical structural traits, not guaranteed visible in every photograph. Sources: https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/oenothera-speciosa/ and https://w3.biosci.utexas.edu/prc/K12/pages/Oenothera%20speciosa.html ."
